# Card 1's incremental cache. Keyed on content hash, regenerated on demand,
# and never a source of truth — committing it would let a stale record survive
# a change, which is the one thing the content-hash key exists to prevent.
.cascade_map/

# Read-only copies of the analysis target — never committed.
target_engine/
target_versions/
.venv-target/

.venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
*.egg-info/
build/
# `dist/*` not `dist/`: git does NOT descend into an excluded DIRECTORY, so a
# `!dist/...` exception under `dist/` never fires. The old deliverable was
# tracked only because it had been force-added once; the exception below was
# decorative. Excluding the CONTENTS instead lets the negation work.
dist/*

# ...except the deliverable: the whole tool in one file, generated by
# `tools/amalgamate.py` and checked by `tests/test_amalgamate.py` against the
# package's output. It is committed because it is what an owner downloads and
# runs; it lives under `dist/` rather than at the root so it cannot shadow the
# `cascade_map` package for anything run from here.
#
# The pattern is a GLOB, not the current filename. Renaming the build output
# to Metatron_Engine_Prototype_v1.py silently un-tracked it: `dist/` is
# ignored, the exception still named `cascade_map.py`, and the file an owner
# downloads stopped being in version control at all. Nothing failed, nothing
# warned -- `git status` simply said clean. A glob cannot go stale that way.
# tests/test_dist_is_tracked.py fails the build if the deliverable is ignored.
!dist/*.py
